## Purpose
- Capture DB ownership and migration workflow for auth service.

## Source of truth
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Data/AuthDbContext.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Migrations/*.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/appsettings*.json`
- `docker-compose.yml` (prod-like stack)
- `docker-compose.dev.yml` (local dev database only)

## Do
- Use PostgreSQL for auth data only (users, roles).
- Use Redis for refresh-token sessions (`auth:session:*`); Redis is shared infrastructure (`infrastructure/redis/`).
- Treat `AuthDbContext` as schema contract (`users`, `roles`, indexes, FK role optional).
- Keep default seeded roles from model config (`admin`, `user`).
- Create schema changes through EF Core migrations in `Migrations/`.
- Keep connection string environment-aware:
  - local dev (`dotnet run`, `appsettings.Development.json`): `Host=localhost;Port=5433;Database=auth_db;Username=matsma`
  - docker auth container (`appsettings.Production.json`): `Host=postgres;Port=5432;Database=authdb;Username=matsma`
- Run dev database via `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d` (`team-hub-dev`, `team-hub-redis-dev`, volume `postgres_data_dev`).
- Run prod-like database via `docker compose up` (`team-hub`, volume `postgres_data_prod`, database `authdb`).

## Don't
- Do not edit migration history manually unless fixing broken migration state.
- Do not use direct SQL schema changes outside migrations.
- Do not assume postgres on host port `5432` for this project setup.

## Checklist
- New/changed model has migration and snapshot update.
- DB startup path still works with `docker-compose.dev.yml` and `docker-compose.yml` postgres services.
- Unique constraints (username) remain intentional.
